### The Services You'll Use in Every Project

AWS has 200+ services, but a typical web application uses about 8-10. Here's what matters.

#### Compute

**EC2 (Elastic Compute Cloud)** — Virtual machines. You choose the OS, instance type (CPU/RAM), and pay by the hour. Use for long-running servers.

**Lambda** — Serverless functions. You upload code; AWS runs it on-demand. Pay only for execution time. No servers to manage. Ideal for event-driven workloads.

```js
// Lambda function (Node.js)
export const handler = async (event) => {
  console.log('Event:', JSON.stringify(event));

  const { userId } = event.queryStringParameters;
  const user = await db.users.findById(userId);

  return {
    statusCode: 200,
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify(user),
  };
};
```

**ECS (Elastic Container Service)** — Run Docker containers without managing Kubernetes. With Fargate, you don't manage servers at all — just specify CPU/RAM and AWS runs the containers.

#### Storage

**S3 (Simple Storage Service)** — Object storage. Unlimited capacity, 11 9s of durability. Store images, videos, backups, static websites.

```js
import { S3Client, PutObjectCommand, GetObjectCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-s3';
import { getSignedUrl } from '@aws-sdk/s3-request-presigner';

const s3 = new S3Client({ region: 'us-east-1' });

// Upload a file
await s3.send(new PutObjectCommand({
  Bucket: 'my-bucket',
  Key: `uploads/${userId}/${filename}`,
  Body: fileBuffer,
  ContentType: mimeType,
}));

// Generate a pre-signed URL (let clients upload directly without going through your server)
const url = await getSignedUrl(s3, new PutObjectCommand({
  Bucket: 'my-bucket',
  Key: `uploads/${filename}`,
}), { expiresIn: 3600 }); // URL valid for 1 hour
```

#### Database

**RDS (Relational Database Service)** — Managed PostgreSQL, MySQL, etc. AWS handles backups, failover, patching.

**DynamoDB** — Serverless NoSQL. Single-digit millisecond latency at any scale. Good for simple key-value lookups, leaderboards, session stores.

**ElastiCache** — Managed Redis or Memcached. Use for caching and session storage.

#### Networking

**VPC (Virtual Private Cloud)** — Your private network in AWS. Resources inside a VPC can't be reached from the internet unless you explicitly allow it.

**ALB (Application Load Balancer)** — Distributes HTTP/HTTPS traffic to EC2/ECS. Handles SSL termination, sticky sessions, and path-based routing.

**CloudFront** — AWS's CDN. Cache static assets at edge locations globally. Also terminates SSL and absorbs DDoS attacks.

#### Key Services Summary

| Need | Service |
| :--- | :--- |
| Run containers | ECS with Fargate |
| Serverless functions | Lambda |
| File storage | S3 |
| Managed PostgreSQL | RDS |
| Caching | ElastiCache (Redis) |
| Load balancing | ALB |
| CDN | CloudFront |
| Secrets management | AWS Secrets Manager |

